A LINK REFERENCE DEFINITION THAT IS USED IS RESOLVED INLINE, AND ITS
DEFINITION LINE IS DROPPED.

WHAT md DOES TODAY. The reference link is pasted inline and the definition
disappears. The whole output is one line:

    paragraph [ref](url) here

WHAT IT SHOULD DO. Keep the author's reference style, byte-for-byte: the
`[ref]` link and its `[ref]: url` definition, both unchanged.

    paragraph [ref] here

    [ref]: url

WHY THIS IS DISTINCT FROM `format-keeps-an-unused-link-reference-definition`.
That case (defect 7) loses an UNUSED definition entirely: the URL appears
nowhere else, so it is gone for good. Here the URL survives, but in the wrong
shape and the wrong place. Two things go wrong, not one:

  1. cmark consumes `[ref]: url` into the document reference map and leaves no
     node, so `MarkdownParser.parse` never sees it — the same root cause as
     defect 7.
  2. cmark ALSO resolves the `[ref]` link node to hold `url` inline, and the
     commonmark renderer writes it back as `[ref](url)`.

That second point is why defect 7's fix is necessary but NOT sufficient here.
Writing the definition back out from the source, on its own, would produce:

    paragraph [ref](url) here

    [ref]: url

which is worse than today: the URL is now DUPLICATED — once inline, once in
the definition. To fix this case the formatter must also know the link was
reference-style and render it as `[ref]`, not `[ref](url)`. cmark tracks that
a link came from a reference; the renderer here does not carry it through.

WHY THE EXPECTATION IS PRESERVE, NOT INLINE. `format` normalizes spelling, it
does not rewrite the author's structure. The editing commands splice source to
keep untouched bytes exact (defect 19). A reference definition is a block the
author wrote on purpose, often to reuse one URL from several links. Converting
it to inline, and deleting the definition, changes the document shape and would
duplicate the URL if defect 7 is fixed first. Preserve is the only expectation
consistent with the rest of this suite.

`[ref]: url` IS A DEFINITION, NOT A DROPPED LINK. Proof, as the neighbouring
cases state it: `"[ref]: /url\n\nSee [ref] here.\n"` resolves — cmark pastes
the URL inline because that is all it kept. The colon makes a definition; the
brackets alone are ordinary text.
